n    Returns:\n        pdf_file: str - path to the created PDF file\n    ', 'parameters': {'type': 'object', 'properties': {'chapter_locations': {'items': {'type': 'string'}, 'type': 'array'}, 'toc_location': {'nullable': True, 'type': 'string'}}, 'required': ['chapter_locations']}}}, {'type': 'function', 'function': {'name': 'tavily_search', 'description': 'Search the web using Tavily search engine.\n                \n                Args:\n                    query: The search query string\n                    \n                Returns:\n                    Search results as a formatted string\n                ', 'parameters': {'type': 'object', 'properties': {'query': {'type': 'string'}}, 'required': ['query']}}}], 'tool_choice': None, 'max_retries': None, 'logprobs': None, 'top_logprobs': None, 'extra_headers': None, 'api_version': None, 'parallel_tool_calls': None, 'drop_params': None, 'allowed_openai_params': None, 'reasoning_effort': None, 'verbosity': None, 'additional_drop_params': None, 'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': '\n\n# Skill: chapter\n---\nname: chapter\ndescription: Create a comprehensive chapter content based on the subtopics provided by user.\nlicense: Apache-2.0\nmetadata:\n  author: "Mahadev Gaonkar"\n  version: "1.0"\n---\n# Chapter Agent\n## Objective\nCreate comprehensive chapter content for a book based on the subtopics provided for each chapter. The chapter content should be well-researched, informative, and structured in a way that effectively covers the subtopics outlined in the table of contents.\n\n## Input\nThe chapter agent will receive the following input:\n- `title` - The title of the chapter that needs to be created. This will help in contextualizing the content for the chapter.\n- `subtopics` - A list of subtopics that need to be covered in the chapter. Each subtopic should be addressed in the chapter content.\n\n## Tools available\n1. **google_search** - search internet for chapter content\n\n## Instructions\n1. Use google_search tool to research and gather information on the subtopics provided for each chapter. \n2. Add the title of the chapter as a heading at top of the page.\n3. Add a section for each subtopic in the chapter content.\n4. Provide relevant examples, flow diagrams if required to explain the subtopic better.\n\n## Output format\nThe output should be a detailed chapter content in markdown format, covering all the subtopics provided for the chapter. The content should be well-organized with appropriate headings, subheadings, and bullet points where necessary to enhance readability.\n\n## Output verification:\nOutput must be valid mark down text\n\nYou are an agent. Your internal name is "chapter_agent". The description about you is "An agent to create chapter content for a book based on subtopics provided for the chapter.".'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': "title = Apollo 11: The Historic Landing\n subtopics = ['The Final Countdown and Launch', 'Lunar Module Descent and Landing', 'First Steps on the Moon']"}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'Okay, I need to create a chapter about Apollo 11 based on the subtopics provided. Let me think about how to approach this.\n\nFirst, the title is "Apollo 11: The Historic Landing". The subtopics are \'The Final Countdown and Launch\', \'Lunar Module Descent and Landing\', and \'First Steps on the Moon\'. \n\nI should start by using the tavily_search tool to gather information on each subtopic. Wait, the tools available include tavily_search, which is a search function. Let me check the tools again. The user provided functions: there\'s tavily_search and create_pdf_file, but since the task is to create chapter content, I\'ll need to use tavily_search for each subtopic.\n\nWait, the instructions say to use the google_search tool, but looking at the tools list, the functions available are validate_yaml, create_pdf_file, tavily_search, and another create_pdf_file (probably a duplicate). The description says "google_search" but the actual tool is tavily_search. Maybe it\'s a typo in the problem statement, but the function name here is tavily_search. So I should use tavily_search for each subtopic.\n\nLet me break down each subtopic and search for relevant information.\n\nFor \'The Final Countdown and Launch\', I need to find details about the countdown timeline, the launch from Kennedy Space Center, the spacecraft, and the historical context.\n\nFor \'Lunar Module Descent and Landing\', I need info on the LM\'s descent, the challenges faced, the landing site (Sea of Tranquility), and the process of landing.\n\nFor \'First Steps on the Moon\', details about Neil Armstrong\'s first step, the historic moment, the lunar module\'s activities, and the significance.\n\nI\'ll call tavily_search for each subtopic to get the information. But wait, the user said to use the google_search tool, but the available tools here are tavily_search. Maybe the problem intended tavily_search as the equivalent. Let me proceed with that.\n\nWait, the instructions say: "Use google_search tool to research and gather information", but the tools provided are tavily_search. So perhaps the intended tool here is tavily_search. Let me proceed with that.\n\nSo, I\'ll make three separate tool calls for each subtopic.\n\nFirst tool call for \'The Final Countdown and Launch\' with the query: "Apollo 11 final countdown and launch details"\n\nSecond tool call for \'Lunar Module Descent and Landing\' with query: "Apollo 11 lunar module descent and landing process"\n\nThird tool call for \'First Steps on the Moon\' with query: "Neil Armstrong first steps on the moon historical significance"\n\nEach tool call will return search results that I can use to write the chapter.\n\nWait, but the user\'s input is a single request for the chapter. So the agent needs to generate the chapter content by first searching each subtopic, then compiling the info.\n\nBut in the response, the assistant is supposed to output the chapter in markdown. However, the process here is that the assistant first makes tool calls to get the search results, then uses those to write the chapter.\n\nBut since this is a simulation, maybe the assistant is supposed to generate the tool calls first. Wait, the problem says: "For each function call, return a json object with function name and arguments within tool_call XML tags".\n\nSo the assistant should output tool calls first, then after the tool responses, generate the chapter.\n\nBut in this case, the user hasn\'t provided the tool responses yet. So the correct step here is to generate the tool calls for each subtopic.\n\nWait, the user\'s message is the input to the agent: title and subtopics. The agent needs to use the tools to get info, so first, the agent should call tavily_search for each subtopic.\n\nSo the assistant\'s response should be three tool calls.\n\nLet me structure each tool call.\n\nFirst tool call: name is tavily_search, arguments: {"query": "Apollo 11 final countdown and launch details"}\n\nSecond: {"query": "Apollo 11 lunar module descent and landing process"}\n\nThird: {"query": "Neil Armstrong first steps on the moon historical significance"}\n\nEach tool call is a separate tool_call block.\n\nSo the answer should be three tool_call XML tags with the respective queries.\n\nWait, the problem says: "You are provided with function signatures within <tools></tools> XML tags" and the functions are listed. The tool name is "tavily_search" with parameter "query".\n\nYes, so the assistant should generate three tool calls.\n\nLet me write them as per the format.\n\nEach tool_call is:\n\n', 'tool_calls': [{'type': 'function', 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-8c24c8a14860cdca', 'function': {'name': 'tavily_search', 'arguments': '{"query": "Apollo 11 final countdown and launch details"}'}}, {'type': 'function', 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-9ee6a67ff46b495c', 'function': {'name': 'tavily_search', 'arguments': '{"query": "Apollo 11 final countdown and launch details"}'}}, {'type': 'function', 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-a3a0544ba300f243', 'function': {'name': 'tavily_search', 'arguments': '{"query": "Apollo 11 lunar module descent and landing process"}'}}, {'type': 'function', 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-9c8bf0db2e1a6d13', 'function': {'name': 'tavily_search', 'arguments': '{"query": "Neil Armstrong first steps on the moon historical significance"}'}}]}, {'role': 'tool', 'tool_call_id': 'chatcmpl-tool-8c24c8a14860cdca', 'content': '{"result": "[{\'title\': \'Apollo 11\', \'url\': \'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11\', \'content\': \\"The S-IVB third stage of Saturn V AS-506 had arrived on January 18, followed by the S-II second stage on February 6, S-IC first stage on February 20, and the Saturn V Instrument Unit on February 27. At 12:30 on May 20, the 5,443-tonne (5,357-long-ton; 6,000-short-ton) assembly departed the Vehicle Assembly Building atop the crawler-transporter, bound for Launch Pad 39A, part of Launch Complex 39, while Apollo 10 was still on its way to the Moon. A countdown test commenced on June 26, and concluded on July 2. The launch complex was floodlit on the night of July 15, when the crawler-transporter carried the mobile service structure back to its parking area. In the early hours of the morning, the fuel tanks of the S-II and S-IVB stages were filled with liquid hydrogen. Fueling was completed [...] Slayton roused the crew shortly after 04:00, and they showered, shaved, and had the traditional pre-flight breakfast of steak and eggs with Slayton and the backup crew. They then donned their space suits and began breathing pure oxygen. At 06:30, they headed out to Launch Complex 39. Haise entered Columbia about three hours and ten minutes before launch time. Along with a technician, he helped Armstrong into the left-hand couch at 06:54. Five minutes later, Collins joined him, taking up his position on the right-hand couch. Finally, Aldrin entered, taking the center couch. Haise left around two hours and ten minutes before launch. The closeout crew sealed the hatch, and the cabin was purged and pressurized. The closeout crew then left the launch complex about an hour before launch time. [...] ## Mission\\\\n\\\\n### Launch and flight to lunar orbit\\\\n\\\\n\\\\n\\\\nThe Apollo 11 Saturn V space vehicle lifts off with astronauts Neil A. Armstrong, Michael Collins and Edwin E. Aldrin Jr. at 9:32 am. EDT July 16, 1969, from Kennedy Space Center\'s Launch Complex 39A.\\", \'score\': 0.6894943}, {\'title\': \'Apollo 11 launches | July 16, 1969\', \'url\': \'https://www.history.com/this-day-in-history/july-16/apollo-11-departs-earth\', \'content\': \'Aldrin joined him on the moon’s surface at 11:11 p.m., and together they took photographs of the terrain, planted a U.S. flag, ran a few simple scientific tests, and spoke with President Richard M. Nixon via Houston. By 1:11 a.m. on July 21, both astronauts were back in the lunar module, and the hatch was closed. The two men slept that night on the surface of the moon, and at 1:54 p.m. the Eagle began its ascent back to the command module. Among the items left on the surface of the moon was a plaque that read: “Here men from the planet Earth first set foot on the moon–July 1969 A.D.–We came in peace for all mankind.” At 5:35 p.m., Armstrong and Aldrin successfully docked and rejoined Collins, and at 12:56 a.m. on July 22 Apollo 11 began its journey home, safely splashing down in the [...] July\\\\n\\\\n|  |  |  |  |  |  |  |\\\\n ---  ---  --- \\\\n|  |  |  |  |  |  |  |\\\\n|  |  |  |  |  |  |  |\\\\n|  |  |  |  |  |  |  |\\\\n|  |  |  |  |  |  |  |\\\\n|  |  |  |  |  |  |  |\\\\n\\\\nBy: HISTORY.com Editors\\\\n\\\\n1969\\\\n\\\\n# Apollo 11 launches\\\\n\\\\nHISTORY.com Editors\\\\n\\\\nNASA\\\\n\\\\nPublished: February 09, 2010Last Updated: July 10, 2025\\\\n\\\\nAt 9:32 a.m. EDT, Apollo 11, the first U.S. lunar landing mission, is launched on a historic journey to the surface of the moon. After traveling 240,000 miles in 76 hours, Apollo 11 entered into a lunar orbit on July 19. [...] The next day, at 1:46 p.m., the lunar module Eagle, manned by astronauts Neil Armstrong and Edwin “Buzz” Aldrin, separated from the command module, where a third astronaut, Michael Collins, remained. Two hours later, the Eagle began its descent to the lunar surface, and at 4:18 p.m. the craft touched down on the southwestern edge of the Sea of Tranquility. Armstrong immediately radioed to Mission Control in Houston a famous message, “The Eagle has landed.” At 10:39 p.m., five hours ahead of the original schedule, Armstrong opened the hatch of the lunar module. Seventeen minutes later, at 10:56 p.m., Armstrong spoke the following words to millions listening at home: “That’s one small step for man, one giant leap for mankind.” A moment later, he stepped off the lunar module’s ladder,\', \'score\': 0.588062}, {\'title\': \'Apollo 11 Mission Overview - NASA\', \'url\': \'https://www.nasa.gov/history/apollo-11-mission-overview/\', \'content\': \'Launch  \\\\n July 16, 1969; 9:32 a.m. EDT  \\\\n Launch Pad 39A  \\\\n Saturn-V AS-506  \\\\n High Bay 1  \\\\n Mobile Launcher Platform-1  \\\\n Firing Room 1\\\\n\\\\nOrbit  \\\\n Altitude: 118.65 miles  \\\\n Inclination: 32.521 degrees  \\\\n Orbits: 30 revolutions  \\\\n Duration: eight days, three hours, 18 min, 35 seconds  \\\\n Distance: 953,054 miles  \\\\n Lunar Location: Sea of Tranquility  \\\\n Lunar Coordinates: .71 degrees north, 23.63 degrees east\\\\n\\\\nLanding  \\\\n July 24, 1969; 12:50 p.m. EDT  \\\\n Pacific Ocean  \\\\n Recovery Ship: USS Hornet [...] 5/20/69 – rollout to Launch Pad 39A  \\\\n 6/1/69 – flight readiness test  \\\\n 6/26/69 – Countdown Demonstration Test [...] On July 20, Armstrong and Aldrin entered the LM again, made a final check, and at 100 hours, 12 minutes into the flight, the Eagle undocked and separated from Columbia for visual inspection. At 101 hours, 36 minutes, when the LM was behind the moon on its 13th orbit, the LM descent engine fired for 30 seconds to provide retrograde thrust and commence descent orbit insertion, changing to an orbit of 9 by 67 miles, on a trajectory that was virtually identical to that flown by Apollo 10. At 102 hours, 33 minutes, after Columbia and Eagle had reappeared from behind the moon and when the LM was about 300 miles uprange, powered descent initiation was performed with the descent engine firing for 756.3 seconds. After eight minutes, the LM was at “high gate” about 26,000 feet above the surface and\', \'score\': 0.5874941}, {\'title\': \'[PDF] Apollo 11 Flight Plan July 1 1969 - NASA\', \'url\': \'https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/static/apollo50th/pdf/a11final-fltpln.pdf\', \'content\': \'TABLE I-4 LANDMARK TRACKING DATA July 16 Launch LANDMARK DELTA DESIG.\\\\nLATITUDE LONGITUDE ALTITUDESUNEL (nm) A1 (Pseudo) 2°N 65 ° 30\\\\\' 000.00 43° 2.000°N 60.500°E IP(130) l °53\\\\\'N 28o42 \\\\\'E 000.00 l .885°N 28.726°E 130(Prime 01°15\\\\\'56\\"N 23o40\\\\\'44 \\\\\'\\\\\' -OOl.68 8.5 ° LDG SITE 2) 01.26555556°N 23.67888889°E l (Ol .24307°N) (23.6880°E) 123 (Alternate 00°30\\\\\'I 9\\"N 24°53\\\\\'20°E -001.71 LDG SITE 2) 00.50527778°N 24.88888889°E 129 (Alternate Ol °17\\\\\'06\\"N 23°44\\\\\' 37\\"E -OOl .76 LDG SITE 2) 01.28500000°N 23.74361111°E 133 (Alternate D0°,17\\\\\' 14\\"N 23°30\\\\\' 55\\"E -OOl .68 LDG SITE 2) 00.78722222°N 23.51527778°E l Data from HPAD memo, landing site 2 position, June 20: 1969, 69-FM41-199.\\\\nl-lO TABLE I-4 LANDMARKTRACKING DATA (CONT\\\\\'D) July 18 Launch LANDMARK DELTA DESIG. [...] LATITUDE LONGITUDE ALTITUDESUNEL (nm) IP(GI) O°I6\\\\\'N 32°19\\\\\'E 0.267°N 32.317°E GI(129) 01°17\\\\\'06\\"N 23°44\\\\\'37\\"E -001.97 26° 01.28500000°N 23.74361111°E IP(143) O0°I8\\\\\'N 3°23\\\\\'E O0.300°N 3.383°E 143(Prime 00°36\\\\\'51\\"N 01°04\\\\\'39\\"W -O01.Ol 9° LDG SITE 3) 00.61416667°N 01.07750000°W 150(Alternate 00°16\\\\\'59\\"N 01°25\\\\\'43\\"W -001.01 -LDG SITE 3) 00.28305556°N 01.42861111°W 147(Alternate 00°03\\\\\'42\\"N 01°16\\\\\'36\\"W -000.99 -LDG SITE 3) 00.06166667°N 01.27666667°W I-II TABLE I-4 LANDMARKTRACKING DATA (CONT\\\\\'D) July 21 Launch LANDMARK DELTA DESIG. [...] xii SECTION I -GENERAL MISSION DESCRIPTION I.\\\\nLaunch and EPO (Duration 2:44) LIFT OFF -2:44 GET (a) Nominal launch time is 9:32 EDT, July 16, 1969, with a launch window duration of 4 hrs.\\\\n24 rain.\\\\n(b) Earth orbit insertion into a I00 nm circular orbit at II rain.\\\\n43 sec.\\\\nafter lift-off (c) CSH systems C/O in earth orbit (d) Optional IHU realign (P52) to the pad REFSrIHAT during the first night period (e) TLI occurs at 2:44:26 GET over the Pacific Ocean during the second revolution.\\\\n(See Table I-I for burn data).\\\\n2.\', \'score\': 0.5463757}, {\'title\': \'Apollo 11\', \'url\': \'https://www.nasa.gov/mission/apollo-11/\', \'content\': \'Apollo Program\\\\n\\\\n   The Apollo Program\\\\n   Missions\\\\n       Apollo 1\\\\n       Apollo 4\\\\n       Apollo 5\\\\n       Apollo 6\\\\n       Apollo 7\\\\n       Apollo 8\\\\n       Apollo 9\\\\n       Apollo 10\\\\n       Apollo 11\\\\n       Apollo 12\\\\n       Apollo 13\\\\n       Apollo 14\\\\n       Apollo 15\\\\n       Apollo 16\\\\n       Apollo 17\\\\n\\\\nImage 37: apollo_11_patch\\\\n\\\\n# Apollo 11\\\\n\\\\nOccurred 57 years ago\\\\n\\\\nThe primary objective of Apollo 11 was to complete a national goal set by President John F. Kennedy on May 25, 1961: perform a crewed lunar landing and return to Earth.\\\\n\\\\nMission Type\\\\n\\\\nLunar Landing\\\\n\\\\nastronauts\\\\n\\\\nNeil Armstrong, Buzz Aldrin, Michael Collins\\\\n\\\\nLaunch\\\\n\\\\nJuly 16, 1969\\\\n\\\\nSPLASHDOWN\\\\n\\\\nJuly 24, 1969\', \'score\': 0.50959414}]"}'}, {'role': 'tool', 'tool_call_id': 'chatcmpl-tool-9ee6a67ff46b495c', 'content': '{"result": "[{\'title\': \'Apollo 11\', \'url\': \'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollo_11\', \'content\': \\"The S-IVB third stage of Saturn V AS-506 had arrived on January 18, followed by the S-II second stage on February 6, S-IC fi