at software uploaded on August 29 missed a single character. This put the spacecraft in a steering test mode—usually only done on Earth—which also deactivated the spacecraft’s attitude thrusters. Phobos 1 could no longer orient its solar arrays towards the Sun. When it ran out of battery power, communication with it was lost. The flight software thus contained code that was supposed to be removed after testing on Earth. Moreover, the spacecraft’s operating system had no safeguards against such mistakes, sealing the spacecraft’s fate.\', \'score\': 0.9993175}, {\'title\': \'7 Accidents and Disasters in Spaceflight History | Britannica\', \'url\': \'https://www.britannica.com/story/7-accidents-and-disasters-in-spaceflight-history\', \'content\': \'The second manned lunar expedition, a feat astronaut Charles Conrad called, “a small step for [Neil [Armstrong]]( but…a long one for me,” was not without a few mishaps. As Apollo 12 was beginning to lift off on November 14, 1969, the top of the shuttle was hit by two different lightning strikes that had the potential to compromise the spacecraft and the mission. The first strike was even visible to the spectating audience, creating a stir and concern about the safety of the mission. But despite the scare, it was determined in a quick check of all the spacecraft’s systems that no damage was done to the vehicle, and it set off to the Moon just as planned. It was the return to Earth that caused a little more trouble. As the spacecraft “splashed down” in the ocean during its return to Earth, [...] him, problems with the spacecraft that had been largely ignored became apparent, and the Soyuz 2 mission was halted. The mission control was able to determine that one of the solar panels on Soyuz 1 had not deployed and was limiting the power to the spacecraft dramatically. Equipment that needed the power from this solar panel was malfunctioning, creating difficulties in controlling the vehicle. It was decided that the mission could no