rth system will be used for communication and ranging with NASA and international partner ground stations. An S-band and Ka-band lunar system will provide communication and ranging with missions in cis-lunar space or on the lunar surface. A short-range S-band system will be used to communicate with Visiting Vehicles during rendezvous and proximity\', \'score\': 0.99991035}, {\'title\': \'Lunar Communications: The Future of How We Connect On The Moon\', \'url\': \'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=JQg8lmnIjRI\', \'content\': "we can Leverage The Investments that we\'ve made for Leo and Geo and apply those to a lunar setting and the way we look at the communication systems is really a set of modular scale building blocks uh we have a software-defined radio we have single board Computing we look at Power conditioning and even conversion to hit the different frequencies of interest and then the types of antennas that you might want to use and as you look at these different missions that you\'re going to need to execute in and around the Moon whether it\'s the relay or actually the user on the ground different pieces of these building blocks can be utilized in order to build up the system that that might be needed and so the the modules that we\'ve been investing in for existing missions include all of the digital [...] support different Communications needs for the different missions and so to speak to active phase right it\'s a pretty uh Innovative technology the the individual elements on the active phase array are essentially omnidirectional antennas uh with about a 60 degree ish type field of view but when you array them or use multiple of them together you get the added benefit of you you combine the received power of all of these antennas and on the transmit side you also get a gain because you\'re transmitting all of these antennas the added functionality is that by by varying the phase or the the time delay of the signal to each of these antenna elements I can cause them to constructively add at my intended user which gives me a nice strong Beam at that user while a low power in the noise type [...] point out here is is that the types of antennas that they used were uh the the dishes like this Deployable dish that you see on the Rover just back uh behind into the left of the American flag and and that type of Technology was critical in order to communicate back with Earth uh because a lot of that communication and that time frame was was really point to point and so the communication was a dedicated dish on the earth a very very large aperture even today for lunar missions we\'re looking at seven meter and and most of the time as large as like 18 meter and larger type dishes or reflectors and that there aren\'t a lot of those in the world and so uh to have time using these types of antennas is is a pretty precious resource and then when you\'re operating at a frequency like K Band which", \'score\': 0.99986446}, {\'title\': "[PDF] NASA\'s Lunar Communications and Navigation Architecture", \'url\': \'https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2024/01/lunar-communications-and-navigation-architecture.pdf?emrc=f1a91a\', \'content\': \'feature of the lunar South Pole — is over two times deeper than the Grand Canyon. Nearby regions of interest are challenging for rovers and astronauts to traverse and safely navigate while maintaining reliable communications. Additionally, when Earth is visible from the lunar South Pole, it is always seen near the Moon’s horizon. The lunar terrain can also adversely affect communications signals traveling over it. Direct-to-Earth communications for Gateway and lunar missions will be supported by NASA’s Deep Space Network facilities, NASA’s Near Space Network’s future Lunar Exploration Ground Systems, the European Space Agency’s European Space Tracking (ESTRACK) network, and commercial ground assets. Together, these will provide near-continuous coverage to Gateway and the lunar South Pole [...] The Lunar Communications Relay and Navigation Services project will provide consistent availability, visibility, and higher data rates for surface users. With multiple relays incrementally launched, the project can offer phased and incremental coverage of the South Pole region. Early implementations will also offer preliminary PNT services as a stepping stone to a more robust, dedicated capability beyond the Human Lunar Return segment. C. Surface Communications NASA is pursuing trade studies on different approaches to lunar surface networking to select implementations that best meet the exploration requirements. These potential approaches include: • Adapting NASA’s Space-to-Space Communication System — a two-way communications system designed to provide voice and telemetry data between [...] Beginning with the initial Human Lunar Return segment of the architecture, a variety of interface standards will enable interoperability. These include the LunaNet Interoperability Specification, the International Communication Systems Interoperability Standard, terrestrial wireless cellular standards, and other similar coordination with industry and international partners.[3,4,5] LunaNet is an internationally coordinated framework for lunar interoperability, envisioned as a set of cooperating networks providing communications, navigation, and other services for users on and around the Moon. The LunaNet concept is based on a structure of mutually agreed-upon standards, protocols, and interface requirements that enable interoperability. National Aeronautics and Space Administration 2023\', \'score\': 0.9998148}]'
